---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Cite from '../components/Cite.astro';
import DotGrid from '../components/DotGrid.astro';
import SourceList from '../components/SourceList.astro';
import ShareRow from '../components/ShareRow.astro';
import LevyChart from '../components/LevyChart.astro';
import BarCompare from '../components/BarCompare.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { scores } from '../data/figures';

// Deep links into the Oct. 19, 2026 Farmington School Board video.
const vid = (seconds: number) => `https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qcF6xnfGMWc&t=${seconds}s`;

const ballotText = {
  before:
    '“Shall the Town of FARMINGTON limit property tax growth for FARMINGTON SCHOOL DISTRICT (SAU #61) under RSA 32:5-i? If adopted for a two-year period: (1) the local property tax levy may not grow beyond the prior year’s amount, adjusted for inflation and new construction; (2) SAU central office spending may not exceed 6 percent of total school district appropriations; and (3) bonded capital costs are excluded from both limits. ',
  marked:
    'These caps apply only to administrative operations of the SAU central office and do not affect classroom instruction, school-based services, or other municipal expenditures.',
  after: ' These limits may be overridden as provided in RSA 32:5-i. Adoption requires a three-fifths (3/5) majority vote.”',
};
---
<Base>
  <header class="topbar" slot="header">
    <div class="wrap">
      <a class="brand" href="#top">
        <svg width="26" height="26" viewBox="0 0 64 64" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="64" height="64" rx="12" fill="#14325c"/><path d="M14 46V18h22v6H21v5h13v6H21v11z" fill="#fff"/><circle cx="46" cy="40" r="7" fill="#f2b33d"/></svg>
        <span>Farmington &amp; Question 2</span>
      </a>
      <nav class="topnav" aria-label="Sections">
        <a class="hide-xs" href="#quick">1-minute version</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#farmington">Farmington</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#ballot">The wording</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#other-side">Other side</a>
        <a class="hide-sm" href="#vote">How to vote</a>
        <a href="#sources">Sources</a>
      </nav>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="date-pill"><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>{site.electionDateShort} · Question 2 is on the back of your ballot</p>
        <h1>It’s called a tax cap. It’s also a cap on <em>Farmington’s schools.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Question 2 would limit how much Farmington can raise in property taxes for its schools each year. That limit
          covers the money for the whole school budget, from teachers to buses to special education, not just
          administration. It doesn’t cap your tax bill, and going over the limit would take a 60% vote.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          {site.publisher && <>I’m {site.publisher}, and </>}<strong>I recommend voting No on Question 2.</strong> Here’s why,
          in plain English, with a source for every fact and the other side’s best arguments.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#quick">Read the 1-minute version</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="#farmington">What it means for Farmington</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="glance card" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
        <h2 id="glance-title" class="kicker">Question 2 at a glance</h2>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>2</dt><dd>caps in one yes-or-no question: one on the school property-tax levy, one on administration</dd></div>
          <div><dt>60%</dt><dd>“yes” needed to adopt the cap, and again whenever a budget would need taxes above it</dd></div>
          <div><dt>$360K</dt><dd>first-year gap Farmington’s school leaders project at 3% inflation, before special ed, fuel and transportation</dd></div>
          <div><dt>&lt;6%</dt><dd>Farmington’s central-office share by the district’s estimate (about 5.5–5.7%), already under the limit. The levy cap is the part that would bite.</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <p class="small muted" style="margin:0">
          <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="The law" /> <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board, Oct. 19" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="1:02:29" />
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== QUICK ===================== -->
  <section id="quick" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The 1-minute version</p>
      <h2>Five things to know before you vote</h2>
      <ol class="steps prose">
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It caps the school district’s property-tax levy, not just administration.</strong> Question 2
            bundles two limits into one vote. One caps central-office administration at 6% of the budget. The other caps
            the district’s whole local property-tax levy (everything except building-loan payments), which helps pay
            for teachers, buses, heat and special education. The ballot says “these caps” don’t affect classroom
            instruction, but a co-sponsor, Sen. Dan Innis, told the Keene Sentinel that sentence covers only the 6% cap.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="The law" /> <Cite id="keene-innis" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>It doesn’t cut anyone’s tax bill.</strong> It limits how fast the school share of property taxes
            can grow, to inflation plus new construction. Town, county and state taxes aren’t capped, so your bill can
            still rise. <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" /> <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Farmington’s own estimate: about $360,000 short in year one.</strong> Assuming 3% inflation,
            district leaders estimate the cap would let school property taxes grow about $377,500 for 2027–28. Expected
            increases in staff pay and health insurance (projected to rise 19%) total about $738,300. That’s before special education,
            fuel and transportation. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board video" />
            <a href="#farmington">See the Farmington numbers</a>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Once it’s in, 41 voters can block what 59 want.</strong> Any budget that would need taxes above the
            cap must win 60% “yes.” HB 1300 doesn’t include a way to repeal it early, and the question returns in 2028.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X" /> <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <strong>Farmington voters already have the final say.</strong> The school budget goes to a town-wide ballot
            every March. In 2026, voters rejected the proposed budget, 239–337, and a smaller default budget took
            effect. <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" /> <Cite id="farmington-results-2026" />
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          <strong>In fairness:</strong> supporters’ best case is that property taxes have climbed fast and that caps
          elsewhere have slowed tax growth. I think that in Farmington, where total school spending has grown more slowly
          than prices (though faster per student, as enrollment fell) and voters already say no when they want to, the risk to students is the bigger concern. <a href="#other-side">Read their arguments</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="prose" style="margin:1.25rem 0 0"><strong>Know someone voting in Farmington?</strong> Send them the 1-minute version.</p>
      <ShareRow />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FARMINGTON ===================== -->
  <section id="farmington">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What it means for Farmington</p>
      <h2>Farmington’s own estimate: about $360,000 short in year one</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        At the October 19 School Board meeting, district leaders walked through what the cap would mean for the 2027–28
        budget, the first one it would cover.
      </p>

      <div class="callout bad district-estimate">
        <p>
          Assuming 3% inflation and little new construction, they estimate the cap would let school property taxes grow
          about <strong>$377,500</strong>. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2603)} label="Video, 43:23" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2717)} label="45:17" />
        </p>
        <BarCompare
          label="Farmington School District estimate for 2027-28"
          rows={[
            { label: 'What the cap would allow', value: 377523, display: '$377,523', tone: 'b' },
            { label: 'Expected increases: staff contracts, health insurance (+19% projected), dental', value: 738287, display: '$738,287', tone: 'c' },
          ]}
        />
        <p style="margin-top:1rem">
          That leaves a gap of about <strong>$360,000</strong>. And it doesn’t yet count special education,
          out-of-district placements (students the district pays to send to other schools), utilities, fuel or
          transportation. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="Video, 46:12" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2960)} label="49:20" /> Their conclusion: it’s “pretty safe to say
          should this tax cap be put into place … that we’ll fall short.”
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3754)} label="1:02:34" />
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>Why so tight?</h3>
        <p>
          The 3% applies only to money raised from local property taxes. The rest of Farmington’s $18.1 million school
          budget is paid for by state aid, federal grants and other revenue, which the cap doesn’t touch. So by the
          district’s numbers, if that other money stays flat, the cap would let the budget grow about 2%, while expected
          costs rise about 4%.
          <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" label="2026–27 budget" />
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          These are the district’s preliminary estimates, and they could move either way. The 2% and 4% are my math from
          the district’s figures. Two things aren’t settled. Recent Northeast inflation readings (3.6% in March, 4.2% in
          September) are above 3%, which would shrink the gap. <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" /> But the district started from a
          tax figure of about $12.6 million; if the levy the cap actually builds on is lower, as state tax-rate figures
          suggest it may be, the cap would allow less and the gap would grow. <Cite id="town-report-2025" label="2025 tax rate sheet" />
        </p>
        <blockquote class="quote">
          “I worry that should this go to the ballot, people will believe that if it’s implemented, it won’t have a
          negative impact on students.”
          <footer>
            Superintendent Elizabeth McAndrews, Farmington School Board, Sept. 14, 2026
            <Cite id="school-board-video-0914" label="Video, 35:14" />
          </footer>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Already stretched</h3>
        <p>
          District leaders said Farmington has lost about 11 full-time positions over the last couple of years that
          weren’t replaced. The high school has no music teacher of its own (another staff member covers), and one
          librarian covers two schools. “I
          don’t know how thin we can spread that,” one told the board. “Everyone’s doing extra jobs at this point.”
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3920)} label="Video, 1:05:20" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3992)} label="1:06:32" />
        </p>
        <p>
          A district leader agreed that cuts couldn’t come from special-education services, because each student’s
          written plan, called an IEP, “is a legal document and what is promised in that document has to be delivered.”
          So cuts would fall on regular education. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(4161)} label="Video, 1:09:21" /> Staff is the biggest cost,
          district leaders said, and programs that aren’t graduation requirements, like athletics and theater, would
          get a hard look. No specific cuts have been proposed.
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3790)} label="Video, 1:03:10" />
        </p>
        <p>
          This year, at the January deliberative session, the district had to correct its fallback (“default”) budget,
          adding $700,000 for health insurance increases it was obligated to pay.
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Deliberative session minutes" />
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The “administration” limit probably wouldn’t bite here.</strong> By the district’s estimate,
          Farmington’s central office costs about 5.5–5.7% of the budget, under the 6% limit. The levy cap is the part
          that would bite. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3588)} label="Video, 59:48" />
          <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="1:02:29" />
        </p>

        <h3>Voters already decide</h3>
        <p>
          Farmington votes on its school budget by ballot every March, and voters use that power. In 2026 the proposed
          budget on the ballot was $19.4 million, which the ballot estimated would raise the school tax rate by $3.25 per
          $1,000 of property value. Voters said no, 239–337, and the $18.1 million default budget took effect. They also rejected the
          teachers’ contract, 264–323. <Cite id="farmington-ballot-2026" /> <Cite id="farmington-special-warrant-2026" />
          <Cite id="farmington-results-2026" /> Even so, the default budget was about 4% above the year before, mostly
          for health insurance the district was obligated to pay. <Cite id="farmington-default-budget-2026" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025, voters passed the budget 308–100 and turned down a petitioned school tax cap, 185–228. (The
          deliberative session had raised that cap’s yearly limit from $250,000 to $1 million before the vote.)
          <Cite id="farmington-results-2025" /> <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="Deliberative session minutes" />
        </p>

        <h3>The district’s own money troubles</h3>
        <p>
          Farmington’s school finances have had real problems, and it’s fair to say so. The district’s 2023 audit came
          back without an opinion, meaning the auditors couldn’t say whether the books were accurate, because
          “inadequate or no documentation exists to support material account balances and transactions.”
          <Cite id="farmington-audit-2023" />
        </p>
        <p>
          In 2025–26 the district ran short of money. The board chair called the shortfall the year’s “most frustrating
          challenge,” said the board had made “significant changes to our business department” to strengthen financial
          management, and said those challenges, “coupled with unanticipated healthcare bills,” led to a mid-year
          Reduction in Force: the district’s first layoffs in years, along with a spending freeze.
          <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="Farmington annual report, pp. 29–30" /> A new business administrator was
          due to start in late October 2026. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(395)} label="Video, 6:35" />
        </p>
        <p>
          Cap supporters can fairly point to this as a reason voters want firmer limits. My view: a tax cap didn’t cause
          that shortfall, and a cap won’t fix bookkeeping. Under a cap, any rebuilding above the limit would need a 60%
          vote.
        </p>

        <h3>The cap gives no credit for lean years</h3>
        <p>
          Over the past decade, Farmington’s school tax levy has roughly kept pace with prices: up about 33% from 2016 to
          2025, against 31% Northeast inflation. But it swings from year to year, up 20% in 2017 and down 13% in 2018.
          HB 1300 starts each year’s limit from the year before, so room left unused in a lean year is lost. Looking back,
          the levy rose clearly more than the cap would have allowed in 4 of the last 9 years, and narrowly (by about
          $47,000) in a fifth. That’s a look back, not a prediction: under a cap, the district would have budgeted
          differently, for example by not lowering taxes in a good year.
        </p>
      </div>
      <LevyChart>
        <figcaption>
          Red bars mark years the levy grew more than 1 point faster than the cap’s inflation figure plus my estimate of
          what new construction could have added; “Close” marks a year it was over by less than 1 point. Levy: state-approved tax rate sheets in Farmington’s town reports
          <Cite id="town-report-2017" /> <Cite id="town-report-2018" /> <Cite id="town-report-2019" />
          <Cite id="town-report-2020" /> <Cite id="town-report-2021" /> <Cite id="town-report-2022" />
          <Cite id="town-report-2023" /> <Cite id="town-report-2024" /> <Cite id="town-report-2025" label="2025" />.
          Inflation: <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" />.
        </figcaption>
      </LevyChart>
      <div class="prose">
        <details>
          <summary>How I figured this</summary>
          <p>
            For each year, I took the change in Farmington’s “local education” tax levy and compared it with the
            inflation figure HB 1300 specifies: the 12-month change in Northeast consumer prices ending in March of the
            prior fiscal year (the March just before the school year the levy paid for). Then I added an estimate of
            what “new construction” could have added: the growth in Farmington’s total taxable value, which was under 1%
            in every year without a town-wide revaluation. For the two revaluation years (2019 and 2024), I generously
            assumed 3%; both were still over. 2020 is a close call: the levy beat the estimate by about $47,000, which a
            new-construction allowance above 0.8% would have covered. The 2016 levy is approximate (the 2016 school tax
            rate times that year’s total assessed value). Reading the law to use March a year earlier gives 5 of 9 years
            over, so my reading is the cautious one. Farmington’s school district has no bond payments, so HB 1300’s
            exclusion for building debt doesn’t change the result. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII" />
            <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3420)} label="Video, 57:00" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How Farmington’s spending has changed since 2016</summary>
          <p>
            From 2016 to 2024, Farmington’s day-to-day school spending rose about 10%, while consumer prices rose about
            30%. <Cite id="census-f33-2016" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024" /> <Cite id="bls-cpi-us" /> Enrollment fell by
            nearly a quarter, so spending per student rose 43%. That’s faster than prices but slower than the statewide
            increase of 50%. In 2024 Farmington spent about the state average per student, and less than about 7 in 10
            New Hampshire school districts.
            <Cite id="census-f33-2016-states" /> <Cite id="census-f33-2024-states" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Farmington’s families and tax base</summary>
          <p>
            Farmington’s median household income is $66,943, compared with $99,031 statewide, so tax increases hit harder
            here. That’s a real reason some neighbors support a cap. <Cite id="acs-income-farmington" />
            <Cite id="acs-income-nh" /> It also means more kids rely on school: about 4 in 10 Farmington students qualify
            for free or reduced-price lunch, compared with about 1 in 4 statewide.
            <Cite id="nces-lunch-farmington" /> <Cite id="nces-lunch-nh" /> The state puts Farmington in its top school
            building aid tier, reserved for “less affluent communities.” <Cite id="nhed-building-aid" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Test scores, 2018–2026</summary>
          <p>
            Farmington students have long scored below the state average. Between 2021 and 2024 they closed most of that
            gap in math and reading, and in science they edged past the state in 2022 (by 1 point) and beat it in 2023 and
            2025.
            <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="Farmington annual report" /> <Cite id="sdganh-scores" /> In spring 2026,
            the same school year as the spending freeze and layoffs, scores fell back: reading went from 49% to 41% of
            students at grade level or above, and science from 45% to 26%, while state averages held steady.
            <Cite id="sdganh-scores" /> <Cite id="keene-2026-scores" /> I include this as the most recent picture of a
            lean year in Farmington’s schools, not as proof of cause. Farmington tests only 140 to 430 students a year,
            and each year tests different kids.
          </p>
          <div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-labelledby="scores-cap">
            <table class="scores">
              <caption id="scores-cap">Percent of students proficient or better (Farmington / state)</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Spring</th><th scope="col" class="num">Math</th><th scope="col" class="num">Reading</th><th scope="col" class="num">Science</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {scores.map((r) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{r.year}</th>
                    {r.year === 2020 ? (
                      <td colspan="3" class="muted">No state tests (COVID-19)</td>
                    ) : (
                      <>
                        <td class="num">{r.math[0]}% / {r.math[1]}%</td>
                        <td class="num">{r.ela[0]}% / {r.ela[1]}%</td>
                        <td class="num">{r.sci[0]}% / {r.sci[1]}%</td>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p class="small muted">
            All tested grades: math and reading in grades 3–8 plus the grade 11 SAT; science in grades 5, 8 and 11. The
            grade 11 SAT changed format in 2024. 2026 results were released September 2, 2026. Sources: the SDGA-NH
            compilation of state results for all years <Cite id="sdganh-scores" /> (the district’s 2025 report shows 2024
            as 40% math and 49% reading), Farmington annual reports <Cite id="farmington-ar-2025" label="2025" />
            <Cite id="farmington-ar-2026" label="2026" />, and state averages for 2018 <Cite id="nhpr-2018-scores" /> and
            2026 math and reading <Cite id="keene-2026-scores" />.
          </p>
        </details>
        <p style="margin-top:1.5rem">
          Hear it from the people who build the budget: the district’s presentation on HB 1300 at the October 19 School
          Board meeting runs about 35 minutes, with questions from residents. <Cite id="school-board-video" label="Watch from 37:21" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== BALLOT ===================== -->
  <section id="ballot" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Read it closely</p>
      <h2>What the ballot says vs. what the law does</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">
          Here is Question 2 exactly as it appears on the back of Farmington’s ballot. Look at the highlighted sentence.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <div class="ballot">
            <div class="q">Statutory question required by HB 1300, Chapter 324, 2026</div>
            <p><strong>2.</strong> {ballotText.before}<mark><span class="sr-only">Highlighted sentence: </span>{ballotText.marked}<span class="sr-only"> (end of highlight)</span></mark>{ballotText.after}</p>
            <div class="choices" aria-hidden="true"><span><span class="oval"></span>Yes</span><span><span class="oval"></span>No</span></div>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            Replica of Question 2 as printed on Farmington’s ballot, with the classroom sentence highlighted. Wording from
            the <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" label="Town of Farmington sample ballot" />. State law sets
            the same wording for every town. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, V" /> Key: SAU = the superintendent’s
            office · appropriations = what voters approve to spend · bonded capital costs = loan payments on school
            buildings · overridden = voted over · three-fifths = 60%.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="stack">
          <caption>Side by side</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">The ballot says</th><th scope="col">The law itself says</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="The ballot says">“These caps apply only to administrative operations of the SAU central office and do not affect classroom instruction, school-based services…”</td>
              <td data-label="The law itself says">
                Only the 6% administration cap excludes classroom instruction. The other cap is on the district’s whole
                property-tax levy, “the estimated amount of local taxes to be raised for the fiscal year attributable to the
                school district,” which helps pay for the entire school budget. If a budget would need more tax money than
                the cap allows, the state “shall not approve any tax rate that exceeds the certified limit” unless 60% of
                voters approve going over it. The law doesn’t say which parts of the budget would be cut.
                <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII, IX, XI & XV" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="The ballot says">“limit property tax growth”</td>
              <td data-label="The law itself says">
                It limits only the school district’s share. It doesn’t limit town, county or state education taxes, so
                your own tax bill can still go up. <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" />
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>So, is it really a “tax cap”?</h3>
          <p>
            Partly. It does cap the total amount the school district can raise in property taxes. But it is
            <strong>not</strong> a cap on your tax bill or your tax rate. It doesn’t cut anyone’s current bill. At most it
            slows how fast the school portion can grow, and it lets the school levy grow with inflation and new
            construction. Even NH Journal, a right-leaning news site, wrote that it “would not guarantee a particular
            limit on an individual homeowner’s bill.” <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Supporters call it the “Taxpayer Protection Act.” <Cite id="housegop-signing" /> A more precise name would be
            a <strong>cap on local school funding</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>The other side, in fairness</h3>
          <p>
            Rep. Ross Berry, a Republican from Weare who proposed the tax-cap amendment and helped write the ballot
            question, says the classroom sentence is accurate because the 6% limit is “the only spending cap that exists.”
            <Cite id="vnews-wording" /> <Cite id="nhb-origin" label="His amendment" /> He argues that because the cap allows
            for inflation, classroom teaching should not be affected. <Cite id="monitor-confusion" />
          </p>
          <p>
            Two taxpayers sued, saying the question is confusing and misleading and bundles two caps into one vote.
            <Cite id="bates-complaint" /> <Cite id="nhb-lawsuit" /> The Superior Court refused to block it, finding they
            hadn’t shown they were likely to win and calling their evidence of classroom effects “speculative and
            uncertain.” <Cite id="superior-order" /> The NH Supreme Court also declined to step in.
            <Cite id="nhsc-order-0917" /> <Cite id="nhpr-court" /> <Cite id="indepthnh-supreme" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== 60% ===================== -->
  <section id="sixty">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The 60% rule</p>
      <h2>Once it’s in, 41 voters can block what 59 want</h2>
      <div class="grid two" style="align-items:center">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            If Question 2 passes in Farmington, any school budget that would need property taxes above the limit must win
            <strong>60% “yes”</strong> on the ballot. A simple majority isn’t enough.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
          </p>
          <p>
            If it falls short, existing law sends Farmington to its default budget, and under HB 1300 the state still
            would not approve a school tax rate above the cap. HB 1300 doesn’t say how default budgets fit under the cap.
            <Cite id="rsa-40-13" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XI" />
          </p>
          <p>
            HB 1300 doesn’t include a way to repeal a cap early. The question comes back on the November 2028 ballot either
            way.
            <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure style="margin:0">
          <DotGrid yes={59} label="100 dots: 59 voters vote yes on a school budget, 41 vote no. Under the cap, the budget still fails." />
          <div class="legend" style="margin-top:.75rem"><span>59 vote yes</span><span class="b">41 vote no</span></div>
          <figcaption>Under the cap, this budget fails.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== OTHER SIDE ===================== -->
  <section id="other-side" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The other side</p>
      <h2>What supporters say, and what to weigh</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        People backing Question 2 have real concerns about the cost of living. Here are their strongest arguments,
        stated fairly, and the facts I think belong next to them.
      </p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="stack">
          <caption>Arguments for the cap</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Supporters say</th><th scope="col">Worth weighing</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Property taxes are climbing too fast.</strong> Statewide, local property taxes rose 47.5% from
                2016 to 2025, or about 12% after inflation. <Cite id="nhfpi-proptax" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                True, and it hurts. But New Hampshire’s state government pays a smaller share of school costs than any
                other state, so more of each increase lands on local property taxes. Question 2 doesn’t change that. It
                only limits the local share. <Cite id="nhb-state-share" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>It isn’t a cut.</strong> The levy can still grow every year with inflation plus new construction.
                Rep. Ross Berry: “I would love to know how a 3.9% property tax increase results in them having to cut
                anything.” <Cite id="vnews-wording" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                In Farmington, property taxes pay only part of the school budget. By my math from the district’s
                figures, even a 3.9% levy increase (about $490,000) works out to under 3% budget growth, short of the
                roughly $738,000 (about 4%) in expected cost increases, mostly contracts and a projected 19%
                health-insurance increase. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(2772)} label="School Board video" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Enrollment is falling while spending rises.</strong> New Hampshire public schools had 160,323
                students in fall 2025, down from more than 205,000 twenty years earlier. <Cite id="keene-enrollment" />
                A free-market research group found spending per student nearly doubled from 2001 to 2024, after
                inflation. <Cite id="jbc-spending" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                AFT-NH, the teachers union, notes that schools still have fixed costs like running buses and heating
                classrooms, and that it’s hard to cut staff when fewer students are spread across many grades.
                <Cite id="keene-enrollment" /> In Farmington, total school spending grew more slowly than
                prices. Per student it rose 43%, faster than prices but slower than the state’s 50%.
                <a href="#farmington">See Farmington’s numbers</a>
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>A November vote lets more people decide.</strong> Early-2025 votes on school-spending caps in eight
                districts drew about 20% as many voters as the 2024 presidential election. <Cite id="monitor-caps-2025" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                That’s a fair point. But once a cap is in place, every vote to go over it happens at Farmington’s regular
                March school election, not in November. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>It puts money in classrooms, not administration.</strong> Americans for Prosperity-NH says the law
                “ensures education dollars are spent in the classroom, not on administrative overhead.”
                <Cite id="keene-innis" /> Statewide, administrator positions grew 78% from 2001 to 2024 while teacher
                positions grew 1%. <Cite id="jbc-spending" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                Farmington’s central office already runs under 6%. The superintendent said it has “not a lot of people”
                and no communications staff: “a big fat zero for us.”
                <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3537)} label="School Board video" /> And studies of older caps in
                other states found no sign that administration absorbed the cuts: in Oregon, administration’s share of
                spending held steady or rose while class sizes grew. <Cite id="figlio-1997" /> <Cite id="figlio-1998" /> A
                newer New York study found cuts spread across instruction, central administration and transportation.
                <Cite id="nguyen-hoang-2022" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Caps do slow tax growth.</strong> After New York capped school levies, average school tax
                increases fell to 1.9% a year in 2012–2018, from 6.6% in 2004–2008 (3.1% in the recession years just
                before). More than 95% of school budgets still passed each year. <Cite id="rockinst-ny" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                That’s the trade-off. A study of New York’s cap found that each $1,000 per student a district lost to it
                lowered test scores, driven by cuts to instruction, teacher hiring and support staff.
                <Cite id="sorensen-2021" />
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td data-label="Supporters say">
                <strong>Most states limit property taxes, and Massachusetts has had a tougher cap since 1980.</strong>
                New Hampshire is one of only seven states with no statewide limit. <Cite id="jbc-firstchance" /> HB 1300’s
                cap is tied to inflation, so it never allows less growth than inflation. <Cite id="jbc-massway" />
              </td>
              <td data-label="Worth weighing">
                Massachusetts’ state government pays about 44% of school funding, compared with 28% in New Hampshire.
                <Cite id="newamerica-ma" /> <Cite id="newamerica-nh" /> And Massachusetts towns can go over their cap with
                a simple majority of voters, once local officials put it on the ballot. HB 1300 requires 60%.
                <Cite id="ma-primer" />
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.25rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What research on tax caps has found</h3>
          <p>
            A 49-state study of older, mostly stricter tax limits found they were linked to more students per teacher,
            lower starting teacher pay and lower test scores, with no sign of lower administrative costs.
            <Cite id="figlio-1997" /> A study of Oregon’s limit found a similar pattern. <Cite id="figlio-1998" /> Other
            studies found little effect on student performance. <Cite id="ddm-1998" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Doubts about caps cross party lines</h3>
          <p>
            In January 2026, 22 House Republicans joined Democrats to narrow a statewide school budget cap bill (HB 675)
            to administrative spending only. Backing that change, Republican Rep. Kristine Perez said a study in her town
            had convinced her that a tax cap is not an ideal way to restrain spending. The changed bill was then killed.
            <Cite id="nhb-hb675" /> Local votes are mixed: in March 2026, voters in nine communities rejected tax-cap
            articles, while Kingston and Lebanon adopted caps. <Cite id="nhpr-rejected" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== VOTE ===================== -->
  <section id="vote">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">How to vote</p>
      <h2>Flip your ballot over</h2>
      <ol class="steps prose">
        <li><p><strong>Vote on {site.electionDate}.</strong> Question 2 is on the general election ballot in every New Hampshire town and city ward. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, I" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Turn your ballot over.</strong> Question 2 is on the back; the front says “VOTE BOTH SIDES.” <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Don’t skip it.</strong> If you leave Question 2 blank, it may not count at all, or it may count against the 60% needed to pass. As of mid-October, the Attorney General’s office hadn’t said which. Mark the choice you mean. <Cite id="nhpr-cuts" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>To keep the cap off Farmington’s schools, fill in “No.”</strong> The cap takes effect only if 60% of voters say yes. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VI" /></p></li>
        <li><p><strong>Questions about where or how to vote?</strong> Contact the Farmington Town Clerk. <Cite id="farmington-clerk" /></p></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          <strong>Few people know much about this yet.</strong> In a September UNH poll, 46% of likely voters statewide said
          they’d vote yes, 33% no, and 21% weren’t sure. Only 8% of Granite Staters said they had heard a lot about it.
          If this page helped you, share it with a neighbor. <Cite id="unh-poll" />
        </p>
      </div>
      <ShareRow />
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="more-divider">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p>
        <strong>That’s the short version.</strong> Below: the math behind the cap, what happened in other New Hampshire
        cities with caps, open questions, an FAQ, and every source.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== MATH ===================== -->
  <section id="math" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The math</p>
      <h2>A limit tied to prices, not to school costs</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lede">The formula in the law is short:</p>
        <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.25rem">
          <p style="font:700 1.15rem/1.5 var(--serif);margin:0">
            Next year’s limit = last year’s school tax levy × (1 + inflation + new construction)
          </p>
          <p class="small muted" style="margin:.5rem 0 0">
            Bond payments for school building projects, once certified by the state, are left out.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII & XVI" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>“Inflation”</strong> means the 12-month change in consumer prices in the Northeast, measured in
            March. The most recent figure, March 2025 to March 2026, was about 3.6%. The one for the first capped year
            isn’t out yet. <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" />
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>“New construction”</strong> means new homes, additions and other physical changes. Rising home
            values don’t count, and neither do town-wide revaluations (when the town reassesses every property).
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII(c)" />
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>What’s missing:</strong> the formula doesn’t adjust for new special-education needs, health
            insurance jumps, or a drop in state or federal aid. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII" />
          </li>
        </ul>
        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            <strong>What the state’s own analysis says.</strong> While lawmakers weighed the bill, the NH Department of
            Education and the state tax agency told the Legislature’s budget office that where districts adopt the cap,
            “local school district revenue and school district expenditures could decrease.”
            <Cite id="hb1300-fiscal-note" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:2rem">The costs schools can’t control</h3>
      <div class="grid three">
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Health insurance</h4>
          <p>
            SchoolCare, which insures nearly 100 New Hampshire school districts and towns, raised rates an average of
            17.4% for July 2026. In Lebanon, for example, plans are written into union contracts, so the district can’t
            easily switch. <Cite id="vnews-health" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>Special education</h4>
          <p>
            Federal law requires districts to provide special education services, no matter the cost.
            <Cite id="nhb-sped" /> Local property taxes paid 83% of special-education costs in 2023–24. In 2024–25, the
            state paid only 66% of the aid it owed for the most expensive cases. It rose to 90% the next year, and the
            state must now pay at least 80%. <Cite id="sped-gap" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h4>State and federal aid</h4>
          <p>
            The cap limits property taxes, not spending. So if state or federal aid drops, property taxes can’t make up
            the difference without a 60% vote. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X & XV" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <p>
          <strong>The yardstick swings.</strong> The cap uses one March inflation number from the year before. In March
          2021 that number was 2.1%, and over the next 12 months Northeast prices rose 7.3%. The next year it ran the
          other way: 7.3%, followed by a 4.3% rise. A single month’s snapshot makes budgets hard to plan.
          <Cite id="bls-cpi-ne" />
        </p>
        <p class="small muted">
          In fairness, one cost is set to dip: the pension contribution rate districts must pay for teachers falls
          slightly, from 19.23% to 18.26% of pay, in the first two years a cap would apply. <Cite id="nhrs-rates" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== NEARBY ===================== -->
  <section id="nearby">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Caps in other NH cities</p>
      <h2>What happened in New Hampshire cities with caps</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Several New Hampshire cities, including Farmington’s Strafford County neighbors Rochester, Somersworth and Dover,
        have had tax caps for years. In years when school costs jumped, their records show position cuts, proposed cuts,
        or votes to go over the cap. In other years, the caps didn’t bind.
      </p>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Rochester</h3>
          <p>
            In 2026, Rochester cut 22 positions from its school budget. The city’s finance director blamed less state and
            federal money, which the minutes record as an “inherent structural deficit in the School Department budget due
            to the reduction of State and Federal funding.” <Cite id="rochester-workshop" />
          </p>
          <p>
            The council used a special exception in the city charter to restore 15 teaching positions.
            <Cite id="rochester-adopted" /> In fairness, Rochester has also often taxed below its cap: $14.5 million below
            what the cap allowed over fiscal years 2021–2025. <Cite id="rochester-below-cap" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Somersworth</h3>
          <p>
            For 2025–26, keeping the same school services meant about a 6% ($2 million) increase. About half was union
            contracts the city approved to stay competitive; the rest included a 14.4% jump in health insurance and
            required special education. Staying within the cap would have meant falling about $1.3 million short, “leading to
            position cuts and possibly a Career Technical Center program cut.” <Cite id="somersworth-workshop" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Franklin</h3>
          <p>
            Franklin’s cap-limited budget for 2026–27 would eliminate more than 16 positions, including an art teacher, a
            music teacher and a computer teacher. The district faced $500,000 more for health insurance, a teachers’
            contract costing over $600,000, nearly $1 million more for special education, and close to $500,000 less in
            state aid. <Cite id="monitor-franklin" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Dover</h3>
          <p>
            Dover’s council went over its cap in 9 of the 18 budget years from fiscal 2009 to 2026, almost entirely for
            schools. In fiscal 2011, a state Supreme Court ruling had set caps aside. And the 2026 overage was mostly loan
            payments on a new athletic complex, a kind of building debt HB 1300 would likely leave out.
            <Cite id="dover-overrides" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout prose">
        <p>
          <strong>One big difference:</strong> in these cities, the elected city council can vote to go over the cap.
          Under HB 1300, going over Farmington’s cap would take 60% of voters at the ballot box.
          <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X(a)" />
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== UNKNOWNS ===================== -->
  <section id="unknowns" class="alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Honest uncertainty</p>
      <h2>What nobody knows yet</h2>
      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>How long it lasts</h3>
          <p>
            The ballot calls it a “two-year period,” and news outlets read the law as ending a 2026 cap after two school
            years, through June 2029. <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" /> But apart from the ballot wording, the law never sets a
            two-year limit. Its only end date is January 1, 2032, and it doesn’t say what a 2028 vote does to a cap already
            in place. <Cite id="rhnh-explainer" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XII & XVII" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>How skipped ballots count</h3>
          <p>
            If you leave Question 2 blank, it may not count at all, or it may count against the 60% needed to pass. As of
            mid-October, the Attorney General’s office hadn’t issued guidance. <Cite id="nhpr-cuts" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Which inflation number applies</h3>
          <p>
            The law uses the March figure “in the prior fiscal year.” I found no state rule yet saying which March. If
            it’s March 2027, that number won’t be published until after Farmington votes on its budget that month, a
            timing problem the lawsuit also raised. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VIII(b)" />
            <Cite id="bates-complaint" label="Lawsuit filing" />
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What gets cut</h3>
          <p>
            The law doesn’t say which parts of a budget must shrink to fit under the cap. That would fall mainly to
            Farmington’s school board, with voters weighing in at the deliberative session and on the ballot.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XI" /> <Cite id="vnews-wording" />
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FAQ ===================== -->
  <section id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Common questions</p>
      <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <details>
          <summary>Will Question 2 lower my property taxes?</summary>
          <p>
            No. It limits how fast the school district’s total property-tax collection can grow. It doesn’t lower what
            you pay now, and it doesn’t limit town, county or state education taxes.
            <Cite id="nhjournal-poll" /> <Cite id="nhpr-explainer" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When would the cap start?</summary>
          <p>
            With the budget for the 2027–28 school year, the one Farmington votes on in March 2027.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XII" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Who decides for Farmington?</summary>
          <p>
            Farmington voters. Farmington’s ballot asks about the Farmington School District (SAU #61). The cap takes
            effect if 60% of voters voting in the district approve it.
            <Cite id="farmington-sample-ballot" /> <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, VI" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What if Farmington’s schools need more than the cap allows?</summary>
          <p>
            Voters would have to approve going over the cap by 60%. If that vote fails, the state won’t approve a school
            tax rate above the cap. The property taxes raised have to fit under it, which usually means cuts unless other
            money fills the gap. <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, X & XI" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Would the 6% administration cap save Farmington money?</summary>
          <p>
            Probably not much. By the district’s estimate, Farmington’s central office costs about 5.5–5.7% of the budget,
            already under the 6% limit. <Cite id="school-board-video" href={vid(3749)} label="School Board video, 1:02:29" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>If it passes and doesn’t work, can we undo it?</summary>
          <p>
            Not right away. HB 1300 doesn’t include a way to repeal a cap early. News outlets read the law as ending a 2026 cap after two
            school years (through June 2029). <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" /> The law itself sets only a final end date of
            January 1, 2032, and it doesn’t say what a 2028 vote does to a cap already in place.
            <Cite id="rsa-32-5-i" label="RSA 32:5-i, XVII" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Couldn’t towns already adopt tax caps?</summary>
          <p>
            Yes. Since 2011, voters have been able to petition for a local cap, which also needs 60% to pass. In 2025 and
            2026 many districts voted on caps, and most rejected them, including Farmington in 2025. HB 1300 puts the
            question on every ballot automatically. <Cite id="nhb-howitworks" /> <Cite id="farmington-results-2025" />
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How did this end up on the ballot?</summary>
          <p>
            HB 1300 began as a bill about congressional districts. In January 2026, Rep. Ross Berry proposed an amendment
            replacing it with a tax-cap question, and the House adopted a revised version in March.
            <Cite id="nhb-origin" /> <Cite id="hb1300-text" label="Read the final law" /> The final version passed the
            House 183–170 and the Senate 15–8, and Gov. Kelly Ayotte signed it on July 15, 2026.
            <Cite id="hb1300-status" />
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
      {site.chat.enabled && (
        <p class="prose chat-only faq-ask">
          <strong>Don’t see your question?</strong>
          <button type="button" class="linklike" data-chat-open data-chat-from="faq">Ask the Question 2 assistant.</button>
          It answers from this page and its sources, and links to each one.
        </p>
      )}

      <h3 style="margin-top:2.5rem">Plain-English glossary</h3>
      <dl class="glossary prose">
        <dt>Levy</dt>
        <dd>The total amount of money the school district raises from property taxes in a year.</dd>
        <dt>Appropriation</dt>
        <dd>The amount voters approve the district to spend.</dd>
        <dt>Default budget</dt>
        <dd>The fallback budget that takes effect when voters reject the proposed one: generally last year’s budget plus required increases.</dd>
        <dt>SAU</dt>
        <dd>School Administrative Unit: the superintendent’s central office. Farmington’s is SAU 61.</dd>
        <dt>Override</dt>
        <dd>A vote to go over the cap. Under HB 1300 it needs 60% “yes.”</dd>
        <dt>Inflation (CPI)</dt>
        <dd>How much everyday prices went up. HB 1300 uses the Northeast Consumer Price Index, measured in March.</dd>
        <dt>New construction</dt>
        <dd>Value added by new buildings and additions. Rising prices of existing homes don’t count.</dd>
        <dt>Bonded capital costs</dt>
        <dd>Loan payments for school buildings, if the state certifies them. These are left out of the cap.</dd>
        <dt>IEP</dt>
        <dd>Individualized Education Program: the written, legally binding plan for a special-education student.</dd>
        <dt>DRA</dt>
        <dd>The NH Department of Revenue Administration, the state tax agency that approves tax rates and would enforce the cap.</dd>
        <dt>RSA</dt>
        <dd>Revised Statutes Annotated: New Hampshire’s state laws. HB 1300 created RSA 32:5-i.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

